  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Prep ingredients: Crush or finely chop 2 garlic cloves. Cut the celery into 1cm chunks. Cut the capsicum into 1cm chunks. Peel the sweet potato, then cut into 1cm chunks. Drain and rinse the mixed beans. Trim the green beans, then cut into 2cm lengths.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/150012/photos/large/DNY_Recipe_Card_New_Illustration_01-3c5e09c19014ea60abcdf05ac61091d4.jpg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Fry aromatics: Heat 1 tbs olive oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Cook the garlic, celery and capsicum, stirring occasionally, for 2 mins or until softened.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/150013/photos/large/DNY_Recipe_Card_New_Illustration_02-2926580d9bbc8c56d65fa9aea6b809f9.jpg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Add tomatoes: Add the sweet potato, barbecue seasoning and 3 tsp plain flour to the pan. Cook, stirring, for 2 mins or until fragrant. Add the mixed beans, tomatoes, 1½ tbs tomato paste, 1½ tbs barbecue sauce and 500ml (2 cups) boiling water. Bring to the boil.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/150014/photos/large/DNY_Recipe_Card_New_Illustration_03-a49ec27f5bf99ddf4ba0511d16af9859.jpg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Simmer gumbo: Cook, stirring occasionally, for 12 mins. Add the green beans and cook for a further 3 mins or until the beans are tender. Remove the pan from the heat. Taste, then season with salt and pepper.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/150015/photos/large/DNY_Recipe_Card_New_Illustration_04-bc759d4f4e2c78354fd94ba1a59b85a9.jpg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Serve up: Divide the gumbo among bowls, scatter over ¼ tsp chilli flakes, if using, and enjoy.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/150016/photos/large/DNY_Recipe_Card_New_Illustration_05-5a7a1690f603c65ff6737795493d279d.jpg"
    },
    {
      "text": "Did you know?: Gumbo is a speciality of Louisiana cooking, although its origins trace back to French, Spanish and African cultures. There are countless versions, some made with seafood or meat while others include okra, which helps to thicken the stew. And that's how the dish got its name – 'gumbo' is derived from the West African word for okra.",
      "imageUrl": "https://marleyspoon.com/media/recipe_card_steps/150017/photos/large/DNY_Recipe_Card_New_Illustration_HACK-4d24763fbe97269c0089cc30f420a7fa.jpg"
    }
  ]
